 "notes": "Controller: damped-least-squares IK on the grasp site. It uses a palm-down hand orientation. A nullspace term pulls the arm toward the home posture, which keeps the elbow off the torso. Orientation weights are soft, so position takes priority.\n\nHand yaw: the controller tries several yaws, at the cube's 90\u00b0 lattice plus offsets of \u00b10.17 and \u00b10.35 rad. For each yaw it dry-runs the whole kinematic path with the same IK. It picks the yaw with the lowest tracking error, joint travel and shoulder-roll penalty.\n\nPhases:\n1. Hover over the target cube.\n2. Descend to grasp with a site offset of (-1, -1, -1) cm in the site frame.\n3. Close the hand slowly.\n4. Lift 9 cm.\n5. Carry, steering on the measured cube-to-destination xy error.\n6. Lower until the cube is 4 mm above the destination top.\n7. Open the hand.\n8. Retreat up.\n\nResults: 40/40 on practice seeds 100000-100039.\n\nWeaknesses:\n- There is no regrasp or retry logic. If the cube slips or tilts in the pinch during the carry, the cube can tip off the destination. This happened in about 10% of runs before the grasp offset was tuned.\n- Targets close to the body (x about 0.17, y about -0.12) are near the reachable limit and rely on the dry-run yaw selection.\n- No collision checking beyond the shoulder-roll heuristic.\n- Sensing noise and different physics are untested.",
